
PART 1 - 

Business Problem - Fraud Detection 

1. What is this problem - Fraud detection is a problem banks are constantly trying to solve. It 
involves flagging accounts that have some sort of suspicious activity on their transactions 
by looking at unusually high payments or an unusual frequency of payments. The goal is to stop 
the fraud from occurring before whatever is stolen can actually be processed by the bank. If 
they ensure the transactions never go through then they have successfully prevented the fraud 
from occurring.

2. Why is this problem important to the organization - Banks rely on convincing their customers that
they can be trusted to store the customers' money safely. If they can not properly identify fraud 
happening, those customers will lose that trust and find a different bank that can protect their money. 
So as well as protecting their own money and investments, eliminating fraud is also a way to draw 
more customers because safety is the primary concern when people are trying to store their money. If
one bank does not have enough security, they can be sure the customer will be able to find another that 
has a much better system. 

3. Who is affected by this problem - The business and the customers are equally affected. THe customers can lose 
their hard earned money without enough fraud protection. This has lead to some people having ruined lives if they 
do not ever get that money back. Also, the business can go under if they let the fraud fester too much in their system.
The government might also have a stake in this because sometimes when banks go under, if they are important enough to the economy 
the government almost has to lend them money to keep them afloat. 


PART 2 -

1. What data would I need - For fraud detection, it would be best to track things like average transaction values, frequency 
of transactions, origin of transactions, and type of items being purchased. By tracking abnormalities in these things, it 
should be able to identify when suspicious transactions are happening.

2. Where could the data come from - Ideally the data would be from past bank transactions and customer purchases over long periods
of time. Along with this, each customer account would be flagged with whether or not fraud was committed and if so, when it was committed
so the model could read the irregularities at the time of the frauds occurrence. 

3. How much historical data is useful - Theoretically as much data as possible over the longest period of time possible is ideal because 
fraud is not exactly a common occurrence and better average spending would be available for longer periods. Customer purchases may fluctuate
in the short term, but will likely average out over long periods. Also with a smaller dataset, there might not be enough fraudulent occurrences 
for the model to get a good idea of what it looks like. 

4. Most important values - The most important variables are the average transaction value and whether or not fraud occurred. It would not be as accurate,
but technically a model could give rudimentary predictions on fraud based on examining if the average spending suddenly shoots up to a high value. Obviously 
without the past determinations of fraud occurring, the model would not know how to predict it at all. 

5. What could be missing or inaccurate - If the customers data has not been recorded over enough time to establish a good average (or usual) value for 
the variables, then the AI might falsely flag fraud for purchases the customer is trying to make. Also, the customer might travel far away from home 
and the AI could falsely flag that as unusual activity.


